1
00:00:08,748 --> 00:00:12,164
How does grey matter give rise
to the felt redness of red?

2
00:00:12,244 --> 00:00:15,728
Many people think it is the
deepest open question we have.

3
00:00:16,528 --> 00:00:19,156
I am John Tan, known as Thusness.

4
00:00:19,236 --> 00:00:23,114
These are my own notes, from my
own practice and contemplation.

5
00:00:23,924 --> 00:00:27,286
In Part 1, I set out our
default mode of thinking.

6
00:00:27,366 --> 00:00:31,552
Faced with anything that appears,
we ask what it is, really.

7
00:00:31,632 --> 00:00:36,009
We look for what it is made of,
mind, matter or some third stuff.

8
00:00:36,089 --> 00:00:38,753
And we cannot rest
until a ground is found.

9
00:00:38,833 --> 00:00:42,875
Taken apart, that turned out
to be a habit, not reality.

10
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:47,028
Now I take the habit to the
place it is felt most sharply.

11
00:00:47,836 --> 00:00:49,785
My claim is simple to state.

12
00:00:49,865 --> 00:00:52,489
Seeing emptiness does
not solve this problem.

13
00:00:52,569 --> 00:00:54,839
It shows a problem that was never there.

14
00:00:54,919 --> 00:00:57,122
Not because the question is too deep.

15
00:00:57,202 --> 00:00:59,599
Because what it stands on is not found.

16
00:01:00,416 --> 00:01:03,044
First, the problem, stated fairly.

17
00:01:03,852 --> 00:01:08,369
The philosopher David Chalmers named
it the hard problem of consciousness.

18
00:01:08,449 --> 00:01:11,630
Science can explain,
at least in principle,

19
00:01:11,710 --> 00:01:16,032
how the brain picks out colours,
stores memories and steers the body.

20
00:01:16,112 --> 00:01:18,179
He called these the easy problems.

21
00:01:18,259 --> 00:01:22,422
Not because they are simple, but because
we know what an answer would look like.

22
00:01:23,224 --> 00:01:26,627
The hard problem is different.
Why is any of this felt?

23
00:01:26,707 --> 00:01:29,167
Why is there something
it is like to see red,

24
00:01:29,247 --> 00:01:31,808
rather than processing
going on in the dark?

25
00:01:32,612 --> 00:01:35,933
Tell the whole story of
wavelengths, cones and neurons.

26
00:01:36,013 --> 00:01:38,487
The felt red still seems left over.

27
00:01:38,567 --> 00:01:42,183
As if it could be taken away, and
the story would stay the same.

28
00:01:42,263 --> 00:01:45,747
Philosophers call this
distance the explanatory gap.

29
00:01:46,560 --> 00:01:50,044
This is a serious question,
asked by careful people.

30
00:01:50,124 --> 00:01:52,644
I do not want to make
it smaller than it is.

31
00:01:53,452 --> 00:01:57,398
Now look at what has to be in place
before the question can be asked.

32
00:01:58,208 --> 00:02:00,986
First, matter must have a fixed nature.

33
00:02:01,066 --> 00:02:03,771
And that nature must
be to lack experience.

34
00:02:03,851 --> 00:02:06,733
Galileo set this out
four hundred years ago.

35
00:02:06,813 --> 00:02:11,570
Tastes, smells and colours, he said,
live only in the one who senses them.

36
00:02:11,650 --> 00:02:14,736
Shape, number and motion belong to bodies.

37
00:02:14,816 --> 00:02:17,344
Physics has been written
that way ever since.

38
00:02:17,424 --> 00:02:20,524
Mass, charge and wavelength
out in the world.

39
00:02:20,604 --> 00:02:22,851
Redness and bitterness somewhere else.

40
00:02:23,652 --> 00:02:26,606
Second, mind must have a fixed nature too.

41
00:02:26,686 --> 00:02:28,848
And that nature must be to be felt.

42
00:02:28,928 --> 00:02:31,230
Inner, private, qualitative.

43
00:02:32,032 --> 00:02:34,714
Grant both, and the question is forced.

44
00:02:34,794 --> 00:02:38,899
How does something whose very nature is
to lack experience produce something whose

45
00:02:38,979 --> 00:02:41,214
very nature is experience?

46
00:02:42,020 --> 00:02:43,683
And it can never be answered.

47
00:02:43,763 --> 00:02:46,306
Nāgārjuna gave the reason long ago.

48
00:02:46,386 --> 00:02:50,531
If something existed by its own
nature, it could not become otherwise.

49
00:02:50,611 --> 00:02:54,326
A nature that turns into its
opposite was never a nature.

50
00:02:55,128 --> 00:02:56,683
So the gap is real.

51
00:02:56,763 --> 00:03:00,175
But only after two fixed
natures have been put in place.

52
00:03:00,255 --> 00:03:02,693
The gap was made by the starting point.

53
00:03:03,508 --> 00:03:07,681
Let me show the same move with something
so ordinary that no one has ever found it

54
00:03:07,761 --> 00:03:08,961
a problem.

55
00:03:09,464 --> 00:03:14,646
Close your hand. There is a
fist. Open it. The fist is gone.

56
00:03:14,726 --> 00:03:16,250
Nobody is puzzled.

57
00:03:16,330 --> 00:03:18,496
Nobody asks where the fist went.

58
00:03:19,308 --> 00:03:20,999
Now let me make it puzzling.

59
00:03:21,079 --> 00:03:23,218
I only need the same two steps.

60
00:03:24,016 --> 00:03:26,726
First, give the hand a fixed nature.

61
00:03:26,806 --> 00:03:30,285
Bones, tendons and skin,
complete in itself,

62
00:03:30,365 --> 00:03:32,772
and by its nature without any fist in it.

63
00:03:32,852 --> 00:03:35,245
List every bone and every angle.

64
00:03:35,325 --> 00:03:37,681
Nowhere in the list is there a fist.

65
00:03:38,492 --> 00:03:41,365
Second, give the fist a fixed nature too.

66
00:03:41,445 --> 00:03:43,117
A thing in its own right.

67
00:03:43,197 --> 00:03:45,907
Hard, compact, able to hit.

68
00:03:46,704 --> 00:03:51,126
Now ask. How does a hand, whose
nature is to have no fist in it,

69
00:03:51,206 --> 00:03:52,770
give rise to a fist?

70
00:03:52,850 --> 00:03:55,270
Where was the fist before the hand closed?

71
00:03:55,350 --> 00:03:57,362
Where does it go when the hand opens?

72
00:03:57,442 --> 00:04:02,131
Describe every bone of the closed hand,
and the fist still seems left over.

73
00:04:02,211 --> 00:04:04,278
There is my explanatory gap.

74
00:04:04,358 --> 00:04:06,574
Call it the hard problem of the fist.

75
00:04:06,654 --> 00:04:09,078
No amount of anatomy will ever bridge it.

76
00:04:09,892 --> 00:04:11,664
Yet nothing was ever missing.

77
00:04:11,744 --> 00:04:13,594
Fist names the hand closed.

78
00:04:13,674 --> 00:04:16,392
It is not a second thing
hiding in the hand.

79
00:04:16,472 --> 00:04:19,640
And the hand is not a thing
whose nature is to lack it.

80
00:04:19,720 --> 00:04:23,734
The problem was made in the
two steps. Just close the hand.

81
00:04:24,536 --> 00:04:26,743
Mind and matter are the same shape.

82
00:04:26,823 --> 00:04:31,322
Define matter as complete, and
by its nature without experience.

83
00:04:31,402 --> 00:04:34,460
Define experience as a
thing in its own right.

84
00:04:34,540 --> 00:04:36,892
Then ask how the one produces the other.

85
00:04:36,972 --> 00:04:40,851
We made it unsolvable, when no
such issue was there to begin with.

86
00:04:41,652 --> 00:04:45,707
One caution. I am not saying
mind is a shape the brain takes,

87
00:04:45,787 --> 00:04:48,383
the way a fist is a shape the hand takes.

88
00:04:48,463 --> 00:04:50,598
That would make matter the floor again.

89
00:04:50,678 --> 00:04:53,411
The hand is singled out
just as the fist is.

90
00:04:53,491 --> 00:04:57,976
The example shows only the shape
of the mistake. Fix two natures.

91
00:04:58,056 --> 00:04:59,570
Then look for the bridge.

92
00:05:00,376 --> 00:05:02,760
Why do the two steps feel so natural?

93
00:05:02,840 --> 00:05:04,594
I set that out in Part 1.

94
00:05:04,674 --> 00:05:08,616
The worldview that there must be things
with natures of their own is in place

95
00:05:08,696 --> 00:05:10,789
before any question is asked.

96
00:05:10,869 --> 00:05:15,984
The hard problem is the sentence, matter
produces mind, turned into a question.

97
00:05:16,796 --> 00:05:20,334
Emptiness is exactly
this. Without essence.

98
00:05:20,414 --> 00:05:21,978
Without a fixed nature.

99
00:05:22,058 --> 00:05:25,185
The old word for a fixed
nature is svabhāva:

100
00:05:25,265 --> 00:05:29,456
what a thing would be from its own
side, apart from its conditions.

101
00:05:30,264 --> 00:05:35,582
Empty does not mean that nothing is
there. The tea is hot. The red is red.

102
00:05:35,662 --> 00:05:36,995
The neurons fire.

103
00:05:37,075 --> 00:05:41,212
What is empty is the nature each one
seemed to carry from its own side.

104
00:05:42,028 --> 00:05:44,099
Turn this on the two boxes.

105
00:05:44,912 --> 00:05:48,247
Matter has no fixed nature
of lacking experience.

106
00:05:48,327 --> 00:05:52,744
Mass, charge and wavelength were never
given apart from what is measured,

107
00:05:52,824 --> 00:05:57,567
seen and reported. They are singled
out, by instruments and agreements,

108
00:05:57,647 --> 00:06:02,391
from what appears. A world complete
without experience is that abstraction,

109
00:06:02,471 --> 00:06:06,648
taken for the ground. Then experience
has to be squeezed out of it.

110
00:06:07,452 --> 00:06:10,787
Mind has no fixed nature
of being felt, either.

111
00:06:10,867 --> 00:06:13,694
Looked for, no inner container is found.

112
00:06:13,774 --> 00:06:16,425
No light switched on before its objects.

113
00:06:16,505 --> 00:06:19,564
Consciousness is always of
something, through something.

114
00:06:19,644 --> 00:06:24,020
The Buddha said that eye consciousness
arises dependent on the eye and forms.

115
00:06:24,100 --> 00:06:26,104
Seeing does not see itself.

116
00:06:26,920 --> 00:06:29,643
So neither side owns its characteristics.

117
00:06:29,723 --> 00:06:31,722
And then there is nothing to cross.

118
00:06:31,802 --> 00:06:36,097
No nature of matter has to be
overcome before experience can appear.

119
00:06:36,177 --> 00:06:38,719
Nothing has to be turned
into its opposite.

120
00:06:38,799 --> 00:06:41,618
Because nothing had a
fixed nature to begin with.

121
00:06:42,428 --> 00:06:46,225
People sometimes hear "without
a fixed nature" as a loss.

122
00:06:46,305 --> 00:06:48,263
As if the world were thinned out.

123
00:06:48,343 --> 00:06:50,070
It is the other way round.

124
00:06:50,150 --> 00:06:52,438
For me, this is the heart of it.

125
00:06:53,232 --> 00:06:55,317
Nāgārjuna put it in one line.

126
00:06:55,397 --> 00:06:59,343
For whom emptiness is possible,
everything is possible.

127
00:07:00,148 --> 00:07:04,497
Because nothing has a fixed nature,
differing characteristics can arise,

128
00:07:04,577 --> 00:07:06,825
each one dependent on its conditions.

129
00:07:07,640 --> 00:07:08,840
Take red.

130
00:07:10,212 --> 00:07:14,851
Felt redness arises with an eye,
light, a surface and attention.

131
00:07:15,664 --> 00:07:19,551
A firing pattern in the visual
cortex arises with electrodes,

132
00:07:19,631 --> 00:07:21,567
instruments and a laboratory.

133
00:07:22,364 --> 00:07:26,251
A wavelength of about six hundred
and fifty nanometres arises with a

134
00:07:26,331 --> 00:07:29,422
spectrometer, and the
theory that defines it.

135
00:07:30,240 --> 00:07:32,515
Each is real within its conditions.

136
00:07:32,595 --> 00:07:36,890
Each is reliable, and each can
be acted on. The painter mixes.

137
00:07:36,970 --> 00:07:40,219
The neurologist treats.
The engineer builds.

138
00:07:40,299 --> 00:07:45,373
None of them is what red really is, with
the others as its effects or its shadows.

139
00:07:46,180 --> 00:07:51,580
So where is the redness? Not in the
rose. Not in the brain. Not in a mind.

140
00:07:51,660 --> 00:07:53,604
It belongs to the coming together.

141
00:07:53,684 --> 00:07:56,390
The way a chord belongs
to no single string.

142
00:07:56,470 --> 00:08:00,479
Pluck one string, and listen for
the chord in it. It is not there.

143
00:08:00,559 --> 00:08:04,247
Yet the chord is not somewhere
else. And it is not nothing.

144
00:08:05,048 --> 00:08:07,432
Now see what the hard problem asks.

145
00:08:07,512 --> 00:08:11,589
It takes one set of characteristics,
the wavelength and the firing,

146
00:08:11,669 --> 00:08:14,293
and treats it as the
owner of a fixed nature.

147
00:08:14,373 --> 00:08:17,771
Then it asks it to produce
another set, the felt red.

148
00:08:17,851 --> 00:08:21,086
When nothing has a fixed
nature, nothing is basic.

149
00:08:21,166 --> 00:08:25,085
And when nothing is basic,
nothing has to be derived from it.

150
00:08:25,884 --> 00:08:28,825
Here is where I part from
most of the answers on offer.

151
00:08:28,905 --> 00:08:30,514
And the parting matters.

152
00:08:31,312 --> 00:08:35,611
Many serious thinkers have seen that
the two fixed natures cause the trouble.

153
00:08:35,691 --> 00:08:39,488
So they change one of them, or
put something new underneath both.

154
00:08:40,292 --> 00:08:44,279
Idealism says all is mind,
and matter is its appearance.

155
00:08:44,359 --> 00:08:47,879
Panpsychism says matter
was experiential all along,

156
00:08:47,959 --> 00:08:49,849
down to its smallest parts.

157
00:08:49,930 --> 00:08:54,143
Neutral monism says one hidden
stuff, neither mental nor physical,

158
00:08:54,223 --> 00:08:59,582
shows up as both. Others put process,
or information, under everything.

159
00:09:00,384 --> 00:09:02,034
I respect each of these.

160
00:09:02,114 --> 00:09:04,914
Each one sees something
true about the old split.

161
00:09:04,994 --> 00:09:08,800
But each keeps a fixed nature
somewhere. It has only moved.

162
00:09:08,880 --> 00:09:13,832
And wherever a fixed nature is kept,
the gap comes back, one level down.

163
00:09:14,644 --> 00:09:18,097
If the base is mind, how does
it give rise to the solid,

164
00:09:18,177 --> 00:09:20,207
shared, measurable world?

165
00:09:20,287 --> 00:09:23,237
If the smallest parts
have tiny experiences,

166
00:09:23,317 --> 00:09:25,316
how do they add up to one felt red?

167
00:09:25,396 --> 00:09:28,835
Panpsychists themselves call
this the combination problem.

168
00:09:28,915 --> 00:09:30,642
The question has not gone.

169
00:09:30,722 --> 00:09:32,562
It has changed address.

170
00:09:33,368 --> 00:09:38,061
Emptiness without replacement puts nothing
in the place the essence was taken from.

171
00:09:38,141 --> 00:09:43,021
Not mind. Not awareness.
Not process. Not a ground.

172
00:09:43,101 --> 00:09:46,458
It removes what was assumed,
and puts nothing in its stead.

173
00:09:46,538 --> 00:09:49,733
That is the only way I know
for the problem to go away,

174
00:09:49,813 --> 00:09:51,598
rather than move somewhere else.

175
00:09:52,404 --> 00:09:55,807
And the clarity of what appears
is not a replacement either.

176
00:09:55,887 --> 00:09:58,089
It is never absent from what appears.

177
00:09:58,169 --> 00:10:00,902
But the moment it is lifted
out and named awareness,

178
00:10:00,982 --> 00:10:03,021
it is one more thing singled out.

179
00:10:03,101 --> 00:10:05,417
Not the stuff everything is made of.

180
00:10:06,232 --> 00:10:11,523
The hard problem is a problem like time
divided into past, present and future.

181
00:10:12,332 --> 00:10:16,047
Cut time into three, and give
each part a nature of its own.

182
00:10:16,127 --> 00:10:19,145
The past kept somewhere.
The future waiting.

183
00:10:19,225 --> 00:10:21,713
The present a thin slice we stand on.

184
00:10:21,793 --> 00:10:24,648
Then ask how the past
passes into the present.

185
00:10:24,728 --> 00:10:26,495
No answer can be found.

186
00:10:26,575 --> 00:10:30,463
If the present had a fixed nature,
it could never become the past.

187
00:10:30,543 --> 00:10:34,389
Nāgārjuna shows that the three times
are defined through each other.

188
00:10:34,469 --> 00:10:37,284
And no time is found
apart from what changes.

189
00:10:37,364 --> 00:10:40,911
Without a fixed nature,
change is simply what happens.

190
00:10:40,991 --> 00:10:42,682
And the clocks still work.

191
00:10:43,488 --> 00:10:47,597
Cut what appears into mind and matter,
and give each a nature of its own.

192
00:10:47,677 --> 00:10:50,287
Then ask how matter gives rise to mind.

193
00:10:50,367 --> 00:10:53,227
No bridge can be found,
for the same reason.

194
00:10:54,028 --> 00:10:59,482
There is a third form. A princess,
Elisabeth of Bohemia, put it to Descartes.

195
00:10:59,562 --> 00:11:04,292
How can a soul that only thinks move
a body that is only extended in space?

196
00:11:04,372 --> 00:11:07,512
Give mind and body
fixed, separate natures,

197
00:11:07,592 --> 00:11:09,564
and a thought can never move an arm.

198
00:11:09,644 --> 00:11:12,906
Without them, the intending
and the moving arise together,

199
00:11:12,986 --> 00:11:14,577
within their conditions.

200
00:11:14,657 --> 00:11:19,428
The doer is named on the doing, and
the doing on the doer. The arm lifts.

201
00:11:19,508 --> 00:11:21,919
Nobody has to cross over to lift it.

202
00:11:22,736 --> 00:11:24,454
All three are the same move.

203
00:11:24,534 --> 00:11:27,823
Fix the natures first.
Then look for the bridge.

204
00:11:27,903 --> 00:11:30,867
In every case, the bridge cannot be found,

205
00:11:30,947 --> 00:11:34,196
because the two sides were
never found standing apart.

206
00:11:34,276 --> 00:11:37,579
And in every case, the
ordinary thing still works.

207
00:11:37,659 --> 00:11:39,332
The train leaves at nine.

208
00:11:39,412 --> 00:11:42,678
The hand reaches for the
cup. The red is seen.

209
00:11:43,476 --> 00:11:46,131
So I do not say the
hard problem is solved.

210
00:11:46,211 --> 00:11:48,160
I say it does not arise.

211
00:11:48,976 --> 00:11:52,637
Someone once asked the Buddha
where a fire goes when it goes out.

212
00:11:52,717 --> 00:11:55,707
North, south, east or west?

213
00:11:55,787 --> 00:11:58,425
The Buddha said, that does not apply.

214
00:11:58,505 --> 00:12:01,373
The fire burned dependent
on grass and sticks.

215
00:12:01,453 --> 00:12:04,376
When they are used up, there
is no where for it to go.

216
00:12:04,456 --> 00:12:06,400
The question was not too hard.

217
00:12:06,480 --> 00:12:09,063
It assumed a fire with
a nature of its own,

218
00:12:09,143 --> 00:12:11,092
which would then have to go somewhere.

219
00:12:11,900 --> 00:12:13,944
How does matter give rise to mind?

220
00:12:14,024 --> 00:12:16,009
That is the same kind of question.

221
00:12:16,089 --> 00:12:18,754
It assumes a matter with
a nature of its own,

222
00:12:18,834 --> 00:12:21,041
which would then have to
produce something else.

223
00:12:21,840 --> 00:12:25,080
What remains is ordinary,
and it stays important.

224
00:12:25,160 --> 00:12:29,196
Science keeps working out which
conditions go with which experiences.

225
00:12:29,276 --> 00:12:31,425
Medicine keeps acting on the brain.

226
00:12:31,505 --> 00:12:33,272
Anaesthesia stops pain.

227
00:12:33,352 --> 00:12:35,392
A stroke can take away speech.

228
00:12:35,472 --> 00:12:37,580
This shows dependence, plainly.

229
00:12:37,660 --> 00:12:41,171
It does not show production by
a brain standing on its own.

230
00:12:41,980 --> 00:12:44,404
This is not a retreat to "all is mind".

231
00:12:44,484 --> 00:12:48,005
And it is not "nothing is
there". The red is seen.

232
00:12:48,085 --> 00:12:50,994
The tea is tasted. The arm lifts.

233
00:12:51,074 --> 00:12:56,406
What is denied is only the fixed nature,
on either side, that the problem needed.

234
00:12:57,200 --> 00:13:01,966
The reasoning shows that the gap was built
into the question. Seeing makes it plain.

235
00:13:02,772 --> 00:13:07,031
Take a sip of tea. Look for the point
where matter turns into feeling.

236
00:13:07,111 --> 00:13:11,256
Where the warm liquid stops being
only matter, and becomes the taste.

237
00:13:11,336 --> 00:13:16,623
There is no such point. There is the
warmth. The bitterness. The swallow.

238
00:13:16,703 --> 00:13:20,482
Nothing stands outside the tasting,
waiting to be turned into it.

239
00:13:20,562 --> 00:13:24,639
And no taster is found behind it,
receiving the tea from the other side of a

240
00:13:24,719 --> 00:13:25,919
gap.

241
00:13:26,320 --> 00:13:29,532
When this is seen, something
more than a question goes.

242
00:13:29,612 --> 00:13:33,704
The mind that took things to have a fixed
nature had a way of looking at everything.

243
00:13:33,784 --> 00:13:35,420
And that way turns too.

244
00:13:36,236 --> 00:13:40,114
Before, appearances looked
mechanical, fixed and rigid.

245
00:13:40,194 --> 00:13:43,538
Matter was dead stuff, pushed
along by other dead stuff.

246
00:13:43,618 --> 00:13:46,704
Mind was a light shut
inside, looking out at it.

247
00:13:46,784 --> 00:13:49,326
Every sight and sound
was a finished thing,

248
00:13:49,406 --> 00:13:53,379
set in its place, waiting to be
explained by something behind it.

249
00:13:54,192 --> 00:13:57,577
After, the same appearances
are met as spontaneous,

250
00:13:57,657 --> 00:13:59,375
and luminously plastic.

251
00:14:00,172 --> 00:14:04,376
Spontaneous, because nothing stands
behind them making them happen.

252
00:14:04,456 --> 00:14:07,696
The red appears whole, just
when its conditions meet.

253
00:14:08,504 --> 00:14:11,703
Luminous, because appearing
is never in the dark.

254
00:14:11,783 --> 00:14:14,828
Every appearance is clear,
known as it appears.

255
00:14:14,908 --> 00:14:18,034
That clarity is not a
light shining from behind.

256
00:14:18,114 --> 00:14:19,941
It is the appearance's own.

257
00:14:20,748 --> 00:14:23,634
Plastic, because nothing
has a fixed nature.

258
00:14:23,714 --> 00:14:26,447
Characteristics are
not locked into things.

259
00:14:26,527 --> 00:14:30,133
They arise, change and fall
away with their conditions.

260
00:14:30,928 --> 00:14:32,822
Plastic does not mean vague.

261
00:14:32,902 --> 00:14:35,037
And it does not mean anything goes.

262
00:14:35,117 --> 00:14:36,912
The red is precise.

263
00:14:36,992 --> 00:14:38,760
The tea can name its garden.

264
00:14:38,840 --> 00:14:40,377
The medicine works.

265
00:14:40,457 --> 00:14:44,806
It is precisely because nothing is fixed
that things can arise exactly as their

266
00:14:44,886 --> 00:14:46,264
conditions allow.

267
00:14:47,060 --> 00:14:51,224
This is Section 3, Part 2 of
the Dependent Arising series.

268
00:14:52,032 --> 00:14:54,361
The world does not lose its order.

269
00:14:54,441 --> 00:14:56,186
It loses its rigidity.

